course: dictionary
lesson: 9999
title: Short and telegraphic
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Telegraphic comes from the telegraph, an old machine that sent messages along wires as electric signals. A telegram was short, so telegraphic writing is very short and leaves out little words. «Arrive Monday stop» is a telegram: it means "I will arrive on Monday."
explain: Telegraphic speech works the same way. It keeps the key words and drops small words such as "the", "a" and "is". «Me want biscuit!» is telegraphic speech: a young child puts key words together and the little words are missing.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. If a sentence is very short and has little words missing, it may be telegraphic. Ask yourself: did the writer leave out small words on purpose to be brief? If yes, telegraphic fits.
fact: Telegraphic means to do with the telegraph, an old machine that sent messages along wires.
fact: Telegraphic speech or writing is very short and leaves out little words.
fact: «Me want biscuit!» is telegraphic speech, and it is normal for young children to talk like this around age 2.
fact: «Arrive Monday stop» is a telegraphic message, with the little words left out.
fact: Telegraphic style is a short, bare way of writing, and it can be used on purpose.
exercise: «Me want biscuit!» is an example of telegraphic speech. What does telegraphic mean here? | To do with the weather | Long and full of little words | *Short and leaves out little words | Loud and cheerful
exercise: Read the telegram «Arrive Monday stop». Why is it called telegraphic? | It is very long and polite | *It is very short and leaves out little words | It is written in a foreign language | It is a poem with rhymes
exercise: The smell is «the fine filament, the telegraphic current». What does telegraphic current suggest? | A warm breeze on a hill | A loud brass band | A thick rope on a ship | *A thin signal, like a telegraph wire
test: Which word means to do with the old machine that sent messages along wires? | *Telegraphic | Telescope | Photographic | Telephone
test: Which sentence is telegraphic? | *Daddy go car. | Please could you pass the salt, thank you very much? | The children are going to the park after school. | It was a bright, sunny morning in the village.
exam: Which of these best describes telegraphic speech or writing? | Quiet and slow, with long pauses | Long, flowery and full of descriptions | *Very short, leaving out little words like «Me want biscuit!» | Written in rhyme with lots of adjectives
